Remember that Lojban claims to be ambiguity- free in only two respects: eve=
ry Lojban utterance can be uniquely decomposed into words, whose syntactic =
type is fixed, and every sentence that passes the official grammar has only=
 one parse tree.   This not a trivial claim, since most languages, includin=
g much simpler conlangs, do not have either of these properties. But when w=
e move on to semantics, this is not enough to guarantee unique readings.  L=
ojban is clear on transformation involving conjunction relations between tw=
o or more sentences with common elements, but not so much on deep structure=
 below the sentence level.  In particular, tanru are opaque, ambiguous,  th=
ough other, less common, structure are as well.
